            {
                "entry_id": "602e3bdf-b2d6-3bc0-a3fc-eed6a3b55f0d",
                "word": "cony",
                "pos": "noun",
                "lang_code": "en",
                "forms": [
                    {
                        "form_id": "f85c8c71-4a7b-46e6-a373-4dc73b9a5e37",
                        "entry_id": "602e3bdf-b2d6-3bc0-a3fc-eed6a3b55f0d",
                        "tags": [
                            "plural"
                        ],
                        "form": "conies"
                    }
                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "24106928-c890-30dd-bd3d-ef864c4c792a",
                        "entry_id": "602e3bdf-b2d6-3bc0-a3fc-eed6a3b55f0d",
                        "glosses": [
                            "A rabbit, especially the European rabbit, Oryctolagus cuniculus (formerly known as Lepus cuniculus)."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "4dedd5f4-8e03-40fd-88cd-8bc3ff93c0a2",
                                "sense_id": "24106928-c890-30dd-bd3d-ef864c4c792a",
                                "text": "I were skynnes of conny, / That causeth I loke so donny.",
                                "translation": "I wear skins of cony, / That cause me to look so dunny.",
                                "english": "I wear skins of cony, / That cause me to look so dunny."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "3677dfb3-094c-44bc-848a-5190c0c3ff3d",
                                "sense_id": "24106928-c890-30dd-bd3d-ef864c4c792a",
                                "text": "ORLANDO. Are you native of this place? / ROSALIND. As the cony that you see / dwell where she is kindled."
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "d70322c8-4c05-3123-b32f-32887cdf7674",
                        "entry_id": "602e3bdf-b2d6-3bc0-a3fc-eed6a3b55f0d",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Rabbit fur."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "UK",
                            "dialectal"
                        ],
                        "examples": []
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "43687725-2651-3994-be2b-dba7b903b2d1",
                        "entry_id": "602e3bdf-b2d6-3bc0-a3fc-eed6a3b55f0d",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Locally for other rabbit-like or hyrax-like animals, such as the Cape hyrax (das, dassie) or the pika (Ochotona princeps, formerly Lagomys princeps)."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": []
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "27095868-69b8-3e86-b526-9673da1ae35e",
                        "entry_id": "602e3bdf-b2d6-3bc0-a3fc-eed6a3b55f0d",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Locally for other rabbit-like or hyrax-like animals, such as the Cape hyrax (das, dassie) or the pika (Ochotona princeps, formerly Lagomys princeps).",
                            "Used in the Old Testament as a translation of Hebrew שָׁפָן (shafán), thought to be the rock hyrax (Procavia capensis, syn. Hyrax syriacus)."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": []
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "03f9706f-f965-3483-87bd-548fe680c905",
                        "entry_id": "602e3bdf-b2d6-3bc0-a3fc-eed6a3b55f0d",
                        "glosses": [
                            "A simpleton; one who may be taken in by a cony-catcher."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "obsolete"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "f0d83845-2de2-459d-959c-888cacd8f39a",
                                "sense_id": "03f9706f-f965-3483-87bd-548fe680c905",
                                "text": "It is a most simple animal; whence are derived our usual phrases of cony and cony catcher."
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "203098b0-86f8-332b-b8a2-ed231a63ae4a",
                        "entry_id": "602e3bdf-b2d6-3bc0-a3fc-eed6a3b55f0d",
                        "glosses": [
                            "An edible West Indian fish, a grouper given in different sources as: Epinephelus apua, the hind of Bermuda; nigger-fish, Epinephelus punctatus; Cephalopholis fulva."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": []
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "53f41315-30f4-3a8a-80dd-ac84f02d7d7d",
                        "entry_id": "602e3bdf-b2d6-3bc0-a3fc-eed6a3b55f0d",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Any if certain members of family Epinephelidae of Atlantic groupers, such as mutton hamlets, graysby, Cuban coneys, and rooster hinds."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": []
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "359d5da5-83d3-36c2-8646-335e14c3a0a1",
                        "entry_id": "602e3bdf-b2d6-3bc0-a3fc-eed6a3b55f0d",
                        "glosses": [
                            "A burbot."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "UK",
                            "dialectal"
                        ],
                        "examples": []
                    },
